<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Garda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Garda is the largest lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, lying at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built villas on its shores, notably at Sirmione, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its towns were contested by Verona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> ruled most of them from the 15th century. In 1943–1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s Italian Social Republic placed its ministries in lakeside towns, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salo">Salò</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-garda.jpg" alt="Lake Garda" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo by Guido Adler. Upload by Moroder · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Rocca_di_Manerba_del_Garda_con_l%27isola_dei_Conigli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake Garda lies between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> to the west and Verona and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> to the east, cutting into the edge of the Italian Alps, and its shore is divided between the provinces of Brescia, Verona and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a>. It is about 52 kilometres long and 16 kilometres wide, and its shape is typical of a moraine valley formed under a glacier.</p><p>The Sarca is its main tributary, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mincio">Mincio</a>, at Peschiera, its only outlet. In Roman times the lake was called Benacus, and some revered it as a god. The modern name derives from the town of Garda and from a Germanic word meaning place of guard, a trace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>. The mild climate supports olives, lemons and cypresses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The strongest evidence of prehistoric settlement dates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, when stilt houses stood in the lower lake. Romanisation took place mostly in the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE. In 268 CE a Roman army under the future emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-gothicus">Claudius Gothicus</a> won a decisive battle over the Alamanni at the lake. The Roman presence is attested by villas, including the Grottoes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catullus">Catullus</a>.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> the area was divided among the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>, Verona and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trento</a>. In the 13th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaliger</a> family subdued the eastern shore, built the castles of Sirmione, Malcesine and Riva, and completed a fortified line called the Serraglio in 1355. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> gained the eastern shore in 1405 and the western shore in 1426, and the 34 lake municipalities received ample autonomy.</p><p>In 1438 a fleet of six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and twenty-five ships was hauled over Monte Baldo by 2,000 oxen. After 1815 the lake belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-lombardy-venetia">Kingdom of Lombardy-Venetia</a>. In 1943 the Italian Social Republic placed its ministries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salo">Salò</a>, Desenzano and other lake towns, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s headquarters at Gargnano; Salò became its capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Around the lake lie 26 comuni, of which Desenzano del Garda is the most populated. The economy depends largely on tourism, which developed from the end of the 19th century, beginning with Gardone Riviera, where the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriele-dannunzio">Gabriele D'Annunzio</a> built the Vittoriale degli Italiani, now a museum. Gardaland is the biggest Italian theme park, and Riva, Torbole and Campione are known for the winds that attract sailors and windsurfers. The rare carpione, a salmonid fish, is found only in the lake.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Garda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 260 a great raid reached Italy before Gallienus defeated it near Milan, and in 268 Claudius II defeated another army near <b>Lake Garda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the war Venice had hauled a fleet overland to <b>Lake Garda</b>, the Galeas per montes.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-20">20 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It controlled a rich territory of valleys, iron mines and lakes between <b>Lake Garda</b> and Lake Iseo.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-brescia">Commune of Brescia</a></li><li><blockquote>German paratroopers freed Mussolini from captivity on the Gran Sasso, and he proclaimed a new Fascist state, with ministries scattered around <b>Lake Garda</b>, especially at Salò.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-social-republic">Italian Social Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Castiglione delle Stiviere, south of <b>Lake Garda</b>, belonged to a cadet branch of the Gonzaga of Mantua.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-castiglione">Principality of Castiglione</a></li><li><blockquote>It sent forces to spread the revolt to the Brescian valleys and <b>Lake Garda</b> towns, where peasants loyal to Venice and the Church resisted.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-brescia">Republic of Brescia</a></li></ul></section>
